          "type": "mc",
          "stem": "Let \\(t=\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2}\\). Without stating \\(t\\)-results, show that \\(\\dfrac{2\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2}}{1+\\tan^{2}\\dfrac{x}{2}}=\\sin x\\)",
          "difficulty": "medium",
          "options": [
            "\\(\\begin{aligned}&\\dfrac{2\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2}}{1+\\tan^{2}\\dfrac{x}{2}}=\\dfrac{2\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2}}{\\sec^{2}\\dfrac{x}{2}}=2\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2}\\cos^{2}\\dfrac{x}{2}\\\\ &=2\\sin\\dfrac{x}{2}\\cos\\dfrac{x}{2}=\\sin x\\end{aligned}\\)",
            "\\(\\begin{aligned}&\\dfrac{2\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2}}{1+\\tan^{2}\\dfrac{x}{2}}=\\dfrac{2\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2}}{\\sec^{2}\\dfrac{x}{2}}=2\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2}\\sin^{2}\\dfrac{x}{2}\\\\ &=\\dfrac{2\\sin^{3}\\dfrac{x}{2}}{\\cos\\dfrac{x}{2}}\\end{aligned}\\)",
            "\\(\\begin{aligned}&\\dfrac{2\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2}}{1+\\tan^{2}\\dfrac{x}{2}}=\\dfrac{2\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2}}{\\tan^{2}\\dfrac{x}{2}+1}=\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2}\\\\ &\\ne\\sin x\\end{aligned}\\)",
            "\\(\\begin{aligned}&\\dfrac{2\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2}}{1+\\tan^{2}\\dfrac{x}{2}}=2\\tan\\dfrac{x}{2}\\cos\\dfrac{x}{2}\\\\ &=2\\sin\\dfrac{x}{2}=\\sin x\\end{aligned}\\)"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "hint": "Use \\(1+\\tan^{2}\\theta=\\sec^{2}\\theta=\\dfrac{1}{\\cos^{2}\\theta}\\) and \\(\\tan\\theta=\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\\) with \\(\\theta=\\dfrac{x}{2}\\), then the double-angle formula \\(\\sin x=2\\sin\\dfrac{x}{2}\\cos\\dfrac{x}{2}\\).",
